  [FAIL] V7a  3 sigma at 10 mas genuinely requires an arc of ~400 yr, as g04's D4a states   (IT DOES NOT.  On a finer grid the MI-A residual crosses 3 sigma at an arc of ~225 yr (2.0% of the orbit), not ~400 yr.  g04's D4a scanned octaves (25, 50, 100, 200, 400) and quoted the first grid point above 3 sigma; its own printed row at 200 yr already reads 2.85 sigma.  This overstates the required arc by roughly a factor 2 -- in the direction of a STRONGER negative, so it is conservative for the conclusion but wrong as a number.  The qualitative statement (centuries, not decades) survives)

==================================================================================================================
VERDICT OF THIS AUDIT
==================================================================================================================

 CONFIRMED.  Sedna's MI-A precession channel falls short.  chi_post = 0.0047 sigma at 100 mas and
 0.047 sigma at 10 mas (canonical), 0.0101 / 0.101 (alt), reproducing g04's table
 to within its printed precision.  The mutation controls confirm the projector is not manufacturing the
 negative (V4a: in-family absorbed 1e+15x, out-of-family 2e+01x).  25 yr really is
 0.22% of the orbit and the 4-element family really does eat a factor 20 of the signal.

 REFUTED, THE CAUSAL CLAUSE (V3a).  "The reason is STRUCTURAL, NOT INSTRUMENTAL" inverts the ranking at the
 headline 100 mas canonical footing -- the one that carries the quoted 0.005 sigma and 639x.  There the
 instrumental factor is 32x and the structural factor 20x.  With the 4-element fit switched off
 entirely the signal is still only 0.094 sigma, 32x short of 3.  The correct statement is: at 100 mas
 the shortfall is mostly instrumental (32x) with a factor 20 of orbit-fit absorption on top; at
 10 mas the absorption becomes the larger single factor (20x vs 3.2x); at neither precision does
 either factor alone explain the shortfall, and at both precisions either alone would suffice to close it.
 The bottom line is untouched.  The error is in the attribution, not the arithmetic.

 TWO SECONDARY NUMBERS OVERSTATED, BOTH TOWARD A STRONGER NEGATIVE.
  * V7a: 3 sigma at 10 mas is reached at an arc of ~225 yr, not the ~400 yr D4a quotes -- D4a scanned
    octaves and reported the first grid point above 3 sigma while its own 200-yr row already read 2.85.
  * V5a: "more telescope does not help, only more decades do" is not what the numbers say.  On the EXISTING
    25-yr arc, 0.156 mas per epoch reaches 3 sigma -- 64x beyond LSST-class, not centuries away.  The
    epoch-count route is the one genuinely shut (1.6e+06 epochs at 10 mas).

 ONE SCOPE NARROWING, NOT A REFUTATION (V6a).  "MI-A is the most generous reading, and a route shut for the
 most generous is shut for every reading" is false as stated: MI-A is the most generous reading of ROUTE A'S
 EXPONENTIAL kernel, and Sedna's aphelion is at y = 72, DEEP NEWTONIAN, where Milgrom's circular-orbit
 theorem constrains nothing and MI's interpolating behaviour is not fixed by the MG fit.  A free O(a_0)
 anomaly gives 3.2 / 3.9 sigma at 10 mas.  The conclusion is nevertheless rescued, by an argument g04
 does not make: under the same ansatz Mars binds too, forcing nu-1 = y^-p to p > 1.66 and capping Sedna at
 0.040 a_0 = 0.14 sigma.  It remains unestablished for kernels with intrinsic eccentricity dependence.

 WHAT SURVIVES INTACT.  Milgrom's theorem is stated conservatively and is not leaned on anywhere it does not
 apply; the deep-MOND limit is never invoked on Sedna (the full kernel is used at y = 72 to 1e4); and both
 a_0 footings genuinely propagate -- dg differs by 2.15x between footings against an a_0 ratio of
 1.21x, i.e. through the exponential, not decoratively.


